    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Well, we want to keep the Postal Service with a form that it is because it provides really an indispensable public function. That means that they deliver to every single address in America every day. That's service first, first and foremost, and it really goes back to the beginning of the Postal Service when Ben Franklin was the first postmaster general. And our framers said, we need a Postal Service to connect this new country and connect everybody together. And that hasn't changed. Every address needs to have that. But they have to be able to break even. They're running a business. Letters are not being sent like they used to. It's more packages, so they have to change. Right now they're going through a number of those changes and I'm a guy all about change, but I also want to make sure they're doing it in a way that doesn't damage service. I just asked for and the Postmaster General just to put a freeze on some of the changes they were making. Here as we're up here in the upper peninsula that was a real concern with folks up here. The Postal Service was going to shut down a processing center here in the U.P. and all the mail instead of going there was going to go to Wisconsin before it came back to the U.P. even if you're just mailing it from Marquette It's got to go to Wisconsin and then come back and that's not that great of idea, especially in the wintertime. It's really a bad idea. And so we've paused that and said, let's get a better sense of how we make sure, especially our rural areas, the rural areas rely on the Postal Service more than anybody.",
